    "body_en": "<p>A history of a planet’s volcanic activity is contained in its rocks. And Mars’ rocks, or at least many of the meteorites that have made the journey from it to Earth, were telling a contradictory story. They appeared to be highly oxidized. The institutional report frames the development in practical terms and ties it to the broader mission or observing effort.</p><p>That matters because Earth science becomes stronger when local observations can be placed inside a broader physical pattern that spans time and geography. The planet operates as a coupled system in which atmospheric, oceanic, cryospheric and solid-Earth processes interact across timescales from days to millions of years. A measurement that captures one variable at one location and one moment has limited interpretive value until it is embedded in the longer series and wider spatial coverage that allow natural variability to be separated from forced change. And Mars’ rocks, or at least many of the meteorites that have made the journey from it to Earth, were telling a contradictory story. Now, a new paper published in the Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth from planetary scientists Hideharu Kuwahara and Ryoichi Nakada of Ehime University provides an.</p><p>But to understand why, first let’s look a little closer at the history of Mars geology. When the planet first formed four and a half billion years ago, its heavy metals were pulled to the center and formed an iron-rich core, similar to that of Earth.</p><p>More recently, NASA’s InSight mission also confirmed a 150-kilometer deep layer of molten silicate rock directly above the core, what we would typically call a “mantle”. However, evidence from both meteorites and Mars crust samples show them to be extremely oxidized.</p><p>On Mars, it forms all the way at the base of its mantle, right above the heavily reducing core, at pressures around 18 gigapascals, or 180,000 times the atmospheric pressure at. So they also cranked the temperature of the system up to around 2,150 to 2,200 degrees Celsius, and saturated it with metallic iron to try to mimic the “reducing” conditions near.</p><p>The broader interest lies in linking the observation to climatic, geophysical or environmental dynamics that extend well beyond the immediate event or location. Earth science is unusual in that its most important questions operate on timescales that no single research career can observe directly, making the archival record, whether in ice, sediment, rock or satellite data, as important as any new measurement. Results that can be embedded in that record, and that either confirm or challenge the patterns it reveals, carry disproportionate scientific weight.</p><p>Between 11% and 21% of the iron locked inside majorite crystals was oxidized, which was a much higher percentage than the surrounding silicates that had also formed during the. Since Earth is about twice the diameter of Mars, it has a much deeper mantle, which allows a different mineral called bridgmanite to dominate its lower reaches near the core.</p><p>Because the account originates with Universe Today, it functions best as a primary institutional report that is close to the data and operations, not as independent scientific validation. Institutional communications are produced by organizations with legitimate interests in presenting their work in a favorable light, which does not make them unreliable but does make them partial. Details that complicate the narrative, including instrument limitations, unexpected failures and results below projections, tend to be minimized relative to progress messages. Technical documentation and peer-reviewed publications, where they exist, provide the complementary layer that institutional releases cannot substitute.</p><p>The next step is to place the result inside longer time series and to compare it with independent instruments and independent sites. Earth system observations gain most of their interpretive power from network density and temporal depth, not from any single measurement however precise. Model simulations that assimilate the new data will help clarify whether the observation fits comfortably within known natural variability or represents a shift that existing models do not reproduce.</p><p class=\"art-source\"><a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/a-deep-mantle-garnet-might-hold-the-secret-to-ancient-martian-magma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source</a></p>",
